# File naming

A filename must identify what is in the file on its own, without the directory as context. Grep
output, tracebacks and editor tabs all show the basename alone, and a tree full of `router.py`,
`service.py` and `client.py` is unreadable in all three.

```
GOOD  routes/k8s_probe_router.py     logger/log_manager.py
BAD   routes/k8s_probes/router.py    logger/manager.py
```

- `snake_case`. A hyphenated module cannot be imported.
- Repeat the concept even when the directory already states it. The redundancy costs nothing and
  the basename has to stand alone.
- No catch-all modules: `utils.py`, `helpers.py`, `common.py`, `misc.py`. If nothing more specific
  fits, the contents do not belong together.
- One primary export, named after the file: `k8s_probe_router.py` exports `k8s_probe_router`.

## Directory layout

Routers are sibling modules under `routes/` (`feature_flag_router.py`, `k8s_probe_router.py`).
Nest a router domain only when *that* domain grows a second module. Request and response
schemas live in `models/`, not beside the router.

Services and models keep a subdirectory per domain from the start: a use case is
`*_service.py` plus `*_errors.py`, and a domain accumulates schemas
(`models/k8s_probes/k8s_probe_response.py`). Nesting never shortens a filename. The basename
still has to stand alone.

`prometheus/` is an HTTP-layer package, sibling of `routes/` and `middlewares/`. The
manager and each HTTP-aware series are sibling modules under it
(`prometheus_manager.py`). Nest a series only when that series grows a
second module.
